For an enumerable cover of neighbourhoods on a domain, index sets record inclusion among indices; interval presentations in the universal domain yield a subsystem isomorphic to the original domain.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`idxSet`).** At index $n$, the index set comprises those indices $m$ whose neighbourhood sits inside the $n$th neighbourhood.

*Proof.* By the subset relation on the cover. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_idxSet`).** Membership in the index set at $n$ is equivalent to the corresponding inclusion of neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`Yidx`).** The $n$th encoded interval in the universal domain is obtained by the standard split-and-generate construction applied to the index family over all indices.

*Proof.* By the interval sequence operator with the chosen split specification. ∎

**Theorem (`D_mem_e`).** Every neighbourhood in the enumerable cover belongs to the ambient domain.

*Proof.* By the covering hypothesis applied at the defining index. ∎

**Theorem (`e_subset_master`).** Each cover neighbourhood lies below the domain master.

*Proof.* By the sub-master property on witnessed membership. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_inter_of_inter_eq`).** When two cover neighbourhoods intersect in a third, the corresponding index sets intersect in the index set of that third.

*Proof.* By rewriting intersection of index sets as simultaneous inclusions and applying the hypothesis on set intersections. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_zero_eq_univ`).** If every index sits below a fixed index, that index’s index set is the full index universe.

*Proof.* By extensionality using the universal inclusion hypothesis. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_zero`).** The index set at zero is universal when zero names the master.

*Proof.* By the master naming hypothesis and sub-master comparisons. ∎

**Theorem (`self_mem_idxSet`).** Each index belongs to its own index set.

*Proof.* By reflexivity of inclusion. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_subset_iff`).** Inclusion of index sets at $i$ and $j$ is equivalent to inclusion of the corresponding neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* By the self-membership criterion in one direction and transitivity of inclusion in the other. ∎

**Theorem (`univ_nonempty_nat`).** The set of all natural-number indices is nonempty.

*Proof.* Zero witnesses. ∎

**Theorem (`Yidx_empty_or_mem`).** Each encoded interval is either empty or a legal neighbourhood of the universal domain.

*Proof.* By the standard empty-or-membership transfer for interval sequences. ∎

**Theorem (`Yidx_nonempty`).** Each encoded interval is nonempty.

*Proof.* By transferring nonemptiness from the index set at $n$ with witness $n$. ∎

**Theorem (`Yidx_mem`).** Every encoded interval belongs to the universal domain.

*Proof.* By ruling out emptiness. ∎

**Theorem (`Yidx_subset_master`).** Each encoded interval sits below the universal master.

*Proof.* By the interval-sequence sub-master lemma. ∎

**Theorem (`Yidx_zero`).** The interval at index zero equals the universal master.

*Proof.* By the zero-index master law for interval sequences and universality of the index set at zero. ∎

**Theorem (`embed_subset_iff`).** Inclusion of cover neighbourhoods at indices $i$ and $j$ matches inclusion of their encoded intervals.

*Proof.* By the index-set comparison and the subset transfer principle for interval presentations. ∎

**Theorem (`embed_eq_iff`).** Equality of cover neighbourhoods matches equality of encoded intervals.

*Proof.* By antisymmetry of the subset characterizations. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_inter_index_of_dmem`).** When the domain admits the intersection of two cover sets, some index names both the set intersection and the intersection of the encoded intervals.

*Proof.* By the covering map on the intersection and the intersection transfer for interval presentations. ∎

**Definition (`DprimeU`).** The subsystem generated by encoded intervals: a set is admitted when it equals some $Yidx$, with the universal master and intersection law inherited via intersection indices.

*Proof.* By the cover, master, sub-master, and intersection witnesses assembled from embedding and existence lemmas. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_inter_index_of_nonempty`).** Nonempty intersection of encoded intervals yields a common intersection index as above.

*Proof.* By contrapositive transfer of emptiness to index sets and the domain intersection lemma. ∎

**Theorem (`DprimeU_subsystem`).** The encoded-interval system embeds as a subsystem of the universal domain.

*Proof.* By master coincidence, membership of generators, and intersection closure from the nonempty-intersection index. ∎

**Definition (`toD`).** From an element of the encoded subsystem, form a domain element by reading membership through cover indices matching encoded intervals.

*Proof.* By the element axioms using cover, master, intersection, and upward-closure transfer along embeddings. ∎

**Definition (`toDprimeU`).** From a domain element, form an encoded-subsystem element by witnessing encoded intervals from cover indices.

*Proof.* Dually, using intersection indices from domain intersections. ∎

**Definition (`domainIso`).** The two translations are mutually inverse order isomorphisms on ideal elements.

*Proof.* By element extension on both sides and the order characterization via embeddings and cover. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_eq_iff`).** Equality of index sets matches equality of cover neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* By antisymmetry of the subset equivalence. ∎

**Theorem (`idxSet_nonempty`).** Every index set is nonempty.

*Proof.* The index witnesses itself. ∎

**Theorem (`isomorphic_DprimeU`).** The original domain and the encoded-interval subsystem are order-isomorphic.

*Proof.* By packaging the domain isomorphism. ∎
